    <p class="dim small">1 November 2026</p>
    <h1>Nun: The Letter That Writes What It Smells</h1>

    <p>
      As per the Hebrew and Iranian calendars, this month — October 23 to November 21 — is the month of Scorpio. In the Iranian calendar it is the month of Aban. The sun is in Scorpio, so the month's letter is Nun. A month has a letter because the sun spends about a month in each sign, and each sign carries a letter of its own. This month, that letter is Nun — and if you sit with it for a while, it starts to explain itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Scorpio answers to Mars, the planet of drive — of doing what is necessary, of breaking through. Modern astrology adds Pluto beside it, the planet of depths, of what is hidden and what transforms. Between the two of them you get Scorpio's whole character: the push to act, and the depth you cannot see. Nun carries both.
    </p>

    <h2>The Mouth and the Tongue</h2>
    <p>
      To understand this fully, we must understand the face of Nun, and how we pronounce this letter. Hebrew letters emphasise the position of the mouth itself, unlike Arabic's control of the tongue. That is how both sister Semitic languages differ. One believes in posture, and one believes and uses speech more than thought.
    </p>
    <p>
      Well, it depends on the situation — both have to work. An Arab and an Israelite are both Semites. Sometimes, in cases of rapid development, speech is more preferred. However, in planning, we need thought as much as speech. Both have to work in a manner that connects, to create.
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore the Nun of Hebrew, <bdi>ן</bdi>, and the Nun of Arabic, <bdi>نون</bdi>, have the same pronunciation — just one is vocal, and one is position of the mouth. The same sound, living in two bodies. That is the first thing Nun teaches: nothing real belongs to one side only.
    </p>

    <h2>Smell More, Write More</h2>
    <p>
      As we recite this, we understand that Nun is the letter of writing. Arabic astrology science says that: <bdi>قلم</bdi>, the pen. However, Hebrew says its intensity is smell. And the geometrical structure is correct: we smell, we observe more, to write more.
    </p>
    <p>
      We watch content, and we make content. Like we say, inspiration is copying others' work through love — <bdi>לב</bdi>, lev. You listen to Radio Gaga, you talk with heart to people, you talk like you have nothing to lose. So be yourself, and focus on yourself. Hear more — because as in Batman Begins, you become what you fear, to conquer your fear.
    </p>
    <p>
      Mars gives the push, but Nun gives the pen. You cannot write what you have not first taken in. Smell first. Listen first. Then write.
    </p>

    <h2>The White Wall</h2>
    <p>
      So we are channels of new creation through old creation. Nun shows the aspects we don't see, like the story of Rumi in which Greeks and Chinese competed over who could paint a wall better.
    </p>
    <p>
      They put <bdi>پردہ</bdi> — curtains — in between the room. On one side were the Greeks, on the other the Chinese. The Chinese showed their skill of colours and painted, and showed the world colours no one had ever seen before. On the other side, the Greeks were just cleansing the room and painting it pure white.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the judge walked on the Chinese side, and he saw, and he was amazed — something of heaven he had never seen. Suddenly he takes the curtain off, and he got to actual heaven, as the light of white shone on the Chinese wall. The colours just became alive, and it became a real world of possibilities. That is how our dreams are, as Rumi says: all we need is a little sunlight and inspiration to move forward in our life.
    </p>
    <p>
      That white wall is Nun's whole method. It does not paint over anything. It clears, until the light can do the work.
    </p>

    <h2>Between Male and Female</h2>
    <p>
      We need to break our idols and do what is necessary to become an OG. And like letter Nun — as I say, letters are living identity — he's a little bit in between male and female. It's magical. The letter who made movies and made music. It's the letter of creation, as Nun is close to Mem, so Nun sits above Mem.
    </p>

    <h2>The Moon's Letter: Tet</h2>
    <p>
      Moving forward — Tet is our letter of the Moon. The moon is the world which revolves inside the human. The moon is very secret, and we share our moon with really loved ones. And this is obviously feminine energy — it's the woman in men, the soft side men have. And as we say, women struggle in the day and men at night — it's a conflict state.
    </p>
    <p>
      The moon is always bleeding, always diminishing, and always starts from a new point. It breaks, then makes, then again breaks and makes. The same matter, but just glued differently, as the moon sign changes — and today it became Tet from Chet.
    </p>
    <p>
      Let's see what letter Tet says — to be continued.
    </p>

